# UI Cleanup and HeroUI v3 Integration Prompt

## Goal

Improve the existing NestPG interface using the supplied screenshots as the visual reference. Make the manager list, manager create/edit dialog, and sign-in screen clean, structured, responsive, and easy to use. Avoid decorative filler, generic AI-style copy, unnecessary motion, and unrelated redesigns.

## Project constraints

- Work within the existing React, TypeScript, Vite, Tailwind CSS v4, and design-token setup.
- Reuse components already present in the codebase where they fit.
- Use HeroUI v3 components from `@heroui/react` only where they replace an existing control or solve a real UI need: Calendar, Breadcrumbs, Tabs, Pagination, TagGroup, Skeleton, Autocomplete, and Table.
- Before implementing a HeroUI component, read its official documentation:
  - https://heroui.com/en/docs/react/components/calendar.mdx
  - https://heroui.com/en/docs/react/components/breadcrumbs.mdx
  - https://heroui.com/en/docs/react/components/tabs.mdx
  - https://heroui.com/en/docs/react/components/pagination.mdx
  - https://heroui.com/en/docs/react/components/tag-group.mdx
  - https://heroui.com/en/docs/react/components/skeleton.mdx
  - https://heroui.com/en/docs/react/components/autocomplete.mdx
  - https://heroui.com/en/docs/react/components/table.mdx
- Use only documented HeroUI props and compound parts. Compose components using their documented compound APIs.
- Style HeroUI components with Tailwind CSS classes and HeroUI theme variables. Put shared theme overrides in the global CSS file so the theme can be changed in one place.
- Do not add a HeroUI component just to use it. Remove an existing component only when the replacement is appropriate and does not break existing behavior.

## Screenshot issues to address

- The Managers page has excessive empty space and default-looking controls. Use the dashboard layout and shared components consistently, with clear loading, error, and empty states.
- The create/edit manager dialog is too wide and vertically crowded. Group identity fields, keep the permission matrix readable, make the dialog usable on mobile, and keep actions accessible.
- Permission labels and controls must be understandable and keyboard accessible.
- The sign-in card stretches far below its content. Size it to the form content while preserving responsive layout.

## Quality requirements

- Keep pages and forms structured, with clear headings, labels, spacing, and action hierarchy.
- Preserve existing manager creation, permission editing, revocation, login, and routing behavior.
- Handle loading, API errors, empty data, duplicate submissions, and small screens without clipping or overlapping elements.
- Keep changes focused. Do not add placeholder components, demo-only UI, or unnecessary dependencies.
- Run the frontend production build and report any visual checks that could not be completed.
